-\section*{Reproducibility Statement}
 \label{sec:reproducibility}
 
-\textbf{Code and data availability.} All experiment code, raw API response logs, analysis scripts, and frozen analysis code are released in the supplementary material (directories \texttt{code/}, \texttt{data/}, \texttt{analysis/}, \texttt{evidence/}). The evidence manifest (\texttt{evidence/evidence\_manifest.json}) contains SHA-256 checksums for all result files, enabling independent verification of file integrity.
-
-\textbf{Step-by-step reproduction protocol.} (1) \textit{Environment}: Python 3.10+ with \texttt{transformers>=4.40}, \texttt{scipy>=1.10}, \texttt{scikit-learn>=1.3}; set \texttt{PYTHONHASHSEED=0}. (2) \textit{API configuration}: set \texttt{OPENAI\_API\_KEY}, \texttt{DASHSCOPE\_API\_KEY}, and \texttt{DEEPSEEK\_API\_KEY} environment variables. (3) \textit{Execution}: run \texttt{python code/run\_all\_experiments.py --seeds 1-30} to reproduce all conditions; each condition takes approximately 2--3 hours. (4) \textit{Analysis}: run \texttt{python analysis/main\_analysis.py} to reproduce all tables and figures; all scripts use deterministic seeds for bit-exact reproducibility of all randomization and bootstrap procedures.
+\textbf{Frozen evidence.} The numerical checks in this candidate are bound to a frozen evidence bundle with SHA-256 checksums. The audit distinguishes persisted API archives, deterministic reanalysis, and assumptions that remain outside the observed data.
+
+\textbf{Open metadata.} This candidate does not assert that raw API logs, a public code repository, or a release license are already available. Those are submission-time decisions and must be bound to exact artifacts before the manuscript is uploaded.
 
 \section*{Broader Impact and Ethical Considerations}
 \label{sec:ethics}
 
-This work studies calibration dynamics in multi-agent LLM systems. Understanding these limitations is essential for safe and ethical deployment.
-
-\textbf{Ethical implications.} (i) \textit{Self-evaluation fragility}: Model updates can silently invert self-evaluation immunity, creating risks of silent reliability decay in safety-critical systems. Practitioners must treat self-evaluation reliability as version-dependent and re-verify after every model update. (ii) \textit{Calibration fatigue}: Long-horizon multi-agent interactions may produce progressive miscalibration without external signals, creating risks in deployed systems with extended interaction horizons. Continuous calibration monitoring and automatic circuit-breakers are recommended. (iii) \textit{Responsible research}: We explicitly report reproduction failures rather than suppressing them; all code, data, and analysis scripts are released with deterministic seeds and SHA-256 checksums for independent verification.
+This work studies how evaluator coupling affects measurement reliability in TTRL-style systems. The results are conditional on the stated model and should not be read as a certification that any evaluator is universally safe.
+
+\textbf{Evaluation risk.} A model or API update can change the empirical coupling signal without an obvious change to the protocol. Practitioners should therefore bind evaluation results to model snapshots and re-run version-sensitive comparisons instead of treating a single-snapshot result as durable evidence.
+
+\textbf{Responsible reporting.} We preserve negative results, estimator failures, and unresolved assumptions rather than converting them into stronger universal claims. The candidate package also distinguishes verified numerical checks from metadata and independent-review gates that remain open.
 
